module contract;

// Fluent Bit hash-table publication — resource-projection vocabulary version 1.
//
// Card HASH-CONTRACTS. This module declares ONE trusted assumption about ONE
// upstream function, `flb_hash_table_add`, and restricts that assumption to the
// exact branch of the upstream body it was read from. It verifies nothing: the
// C body is not analyzed by this loader, and a declaration is a premise.
//
// ## What the pinned body does (src/flb_hash_table.c at Fluent Bit
// ## 66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97)
//
// `flb_hash_table_add(ht, key, key_len, val, val_size)` reaches the value
// through `entry_set_value(entry, val, val_size)` on both the replacement path
// (line 437) and the fresh-entry path (line 475). `entry_set_value` (line 357)
// has exactly two arms:
//
//   * `val_size > 0`  — `entry->val = flb_malloc(val_size + 1)` and
//     `memcpy(entry->val, val, val_size)`. The table holds a COPY it allocated;
//     `entry->val_size` records the positive size.
//   * otherwise       — `entry->val = val; entry->val_size = -1;`. The table
//     holds the CALLER'S POINTER, with no copy and no allocation. The comment
//     upstream says it: "just do a reference".
//
// `flb_hash_table_entry_free` (line 85) frees `entry->val` under exactly
// `entry->val && entry->val_size > 0`. A referenced value carries `-1`, so the
// table NEVER frees a pointer it was handed at size 0, on any teardown path.
//
// ## Which mode is faithful, and why it is not `resource_stored`
//
// `resource_stored(val, ht, ...)` is refused here on two independent measured
// grounds, either of which is fatal:
//
//   1. It is FALSE of the body. §C04.2 reads the stored relation as custody
//      "indexed beneath the designated owner", whose destruction or transfer
//      cascades to the child. This table's teardown does not free a referenced
//      value, so declaring it stored would DELETE the caller's own cleanup
//      obligation for a resource nothing else releases — the exact direction
//      hard invariant 1 forbids.
//   2. It is UNREACHABLE. §C04.2 requires the `owner` parameter to be declared
//      `Owned`. At every real call site the table is reached as a member read
//      of a borrowed instance, so no caller supplies live custody of `ht`, and
//      a declaration requiring it would refuse every call rather than model it.
//
// The faithful mode is therefore `resource_borrowed` on every parameter: the
// call imposes no custody precondition and moves no obligation. That is true on
// BOTH arms — the copy arm touches only storage the callee itself allocated,
// and the reference arm frees nothing — so this declaration deletes no cleanup
// obligation the caller had before the call, which is the whole of what the
// per-exit resource projection reads out of it.
//
// ## What this declaration does NOT say (named, not hidden)
//
// At `val_size == 0` the table RETAINS the caller's pointer for as long as the
// entry lives. This projection models custody, not reachability: it does not
// claim the retained pointer stays valid, does not model the entry's lifetime,
// and does not decide whether a later release of the value leaves the table
// holding a dangling entry. That publication is O00 blockers B03/R13 and stays
// unmodeled. Declaring `resource_borrowed` is a statement about obligations,
// never a statement that the callee forgets the pointer.
//
// This module also says nothing about the table's own key storage, its eviction
// modes, its counters, its locking, or `entry_set_value`'s `size_t` parameter
// receiving an `ssize_t` argument (a negative size reaches the copy arm as a
// very large unsigned length).
//
// ## The size-0 boundary is enforced, not assumed
//
// Everything above was read off the reference arm. The copy arm allocates,
// reads `val_size` bytes through `val`, and can fail — none of which this
// projection modeled. So the declaration must not be believed at a call site
// that takes the other arm. §C04.5's literal-argument premise states exactly
// that boundary: the contract below is applied only where the `val_size` actual
// is exactly the literal `0`, and every other call to this function is refused
// as if it were uncontracted.
//
// The premise is a clause of the declaration itself. The loader validates it
// total-or-reject — a parameter name this signature does not declare, a
// malformed literal or a second premise on one parameter rejects this whole
// module — and it can only ever REFUSE call sites the declaration would
// otherwise have covered. Card HASH-CLAUSE; it replaces the machine-parsed
// comment directive card HASH-CONTRACTS carried here, which is now retired and
// rejected by name.

extern fn flb_hash_table_add(ht: ptr<mut, u8>, key: ptr<const, u8>, key_len: i32, val: ptr<mut, u8>, val_size: isize) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_literal_argument(val_size, 0);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ht);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(key);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(key_len);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(val);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(val_size);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// ## Card W45-CALLEE-CASCADE-RESIDUAL round 3: `flb_hash_table_del`
//
// ONE TRUSTED ASSUMPTION, pinned after reading the body at both revisions
// (`src/flb_hash_table.c:605-674` and its `static inline`
// `flb_hash_table_entry_free`, `:85-97`, byte-identical at `66910c10` and
// `046accf6`). It is the only uncontracted call of the tail-pause slice's
// `flb_tail_scan_unregister_aged_out_inode` and
// `flb_tail_scan_unregister_ignored_file_size` (`plugins/in_tail/tail_scan.c`).
//
// It returns -1 for a NULL or empty `key` or a hash failure; otherwise it
// finds the entry whose key equals `key` in `ht`'s chain and frees it through
// `flb_hash_table_entry_free`, which unlinks it from both lists, decrements
// the counters, frees the table's own key copy, frees `entry->val` only when
// `entry->val_size > 0` (a copy the table allocated in `flb_hash_table_add`'s
// copy arm) and frees the entry. A value the caller added by reference
// (`val_size == 0`, `entry->val_size == -1`) is never freed (the reading of
// `flb_hash_table_add` above). So the call releases only storage the table
// itself allocated and moves no obligation of the caller: `ht` and `key`
// borrowed, the result a status. NOT CLAIMED: the end of the table's
// retention of a referenced value (the same unmodelled publication as
// `flb_hash_table_add`'s, O00 B03/R13).

extern fn flb_hash_table_del(ht: ptr<mut, u8>, key: ptr<const, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ht);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(key);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
